es%20and%20a%20sideboard%20buffet%20to%20keep%20the%20table%20clear.%20%23christmastable%20%23christmasdecor" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" style="display:inline-block;background:#E60023;color:#ffffff;font-weight:700;padding:12px 22px;border-radius:999px;text-decoration:none;">Save to Pinterest</a></div></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Christmas Table Decor Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>A centerpiece that is too tall.</strong> Guests end up talking around it. Keep it under about 12 inches.</li>



<li><strong>Real candles inside dry greenery.</strong> Use flameless candles there, or keep real flames at least 12 inches away from anything that can burn.</li>



<li><strong>No room for food.</strong> Leave a landing strip or serve from a sideboard.</li>



<li><strong>Toxic plants within reach of pets.</strong> The ASPCA lists <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/holly" target="_blank" rel="noopener">holly</a> as toxic to dogs and cats, so keep real holly off low tables or use faux stems.</li>



<li><strong>Food left out all evening.</strong> Put perishable leftovers back in the fridge within 2 hours.</li>



<li><strong>Too many colors and themes.</strong> Pick three colors and repeat them.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Store It for Next Year</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After the holidays, wrap hurricanes in dish towels (tea towels), keep ornaments in divided boxes and store napkins and runners clean and folded. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/christmas-decoration-storage/">Christmas decoration storage</a> ideas and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/linen-closet-organization/">linen closet organization</a> guide show where everything can go, so next year&#8217;s table takes half the time.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Christmas Table Decoration Ideas FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What do you put on a Christmas table?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A runner or tablecloth, a low centerpiece of greenery, fruit or ornaments, candles or string lights, and a simple place setting for each guest with a small natural touch.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How tall should a Christmas centerpiece be?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep it under about 12 inches (30 cm) so guests can see and talk across the table. Tall arrangements work better on a sideboard or buffet.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you decorate a small table for Christmas?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use one low focal point, a narrow runner and simple place settings, skip chargers if space is tight, and serve the food from a counter or sideboard.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Are candles safe on a Christmas table?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Real candles can be used with care: keep them at least 12 inches from anything that can burn, use stable holders and blow them out when you leave the room. Flameless LED candles are the safer choice near greenery and napkins.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best Christmas table decoration ideas are simple: one low centerpiece, a few natural touches, warm light and room for the food. Start with what you already own, add a little greenery and keep the palette calm. If your dining space doubles as storage the rest of the year, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a> can help you clear the table before the holidays.</p>



